 "descripcion": "Advanced English is limited less by what the speaker does not know than by what they reach for first. *Very*, *good*, *bad*, *thing*, *a lot*, *nice*, *interesting*, *make*, *get* and *big* cover an enormous amount of ground badly. Replacing them forces retrieval of vocabulary already known but inaccessible under time pressure. This week the words are simply banned.",
 "ejemplos": [
  {
   "texto": "*very tasty* → *moreish*, *punchy*, *rich*"
  },
  {
   "texto": "*a bad meal* → *bland*, *stodgy*, *overdone*, *forgettable*"
  },
  {
   "texto": "*very expensive* → *steep*, *eye-watering*, *not worth it*"
  },
  {
   "texto": "*It was bland rather than bad — just forgettable.*",
   "audio": true
  },
  {
   "texto": "*The sauce was rich without being cloying.*",
   "audio": true
  },
  {
   "texto": "*Rice is a staple there, not a side dish.*",
   "audio": true
  },
  {
   "texto": "*The prices were eye-watering for what arrived.*",
   "audio": true
  },
  {
   "texto": "*It's an acquired taste, and I haven't acquired it.*",
   "audio": true
  }
 ],
 "notas": [
  "*Very + adjective* usually means a stronger single adjective exists.",
  "Precision is not formality. *Bland* and *stodgy* are everyday words, just specific ones.",
  "*Thing* is almost always avoidable and almost always worth avoiding in writing."
 ],
 "errores": [
  "Swapping an overused word for an obscure one. *Comestible* is no improvement on *edible*.",
  "*Delicious* becoming the new *very good*.",
  "*Interesting* as polite evasion."
 ],
